## Recovery, checkpoint and HTTP findings

I found no new reproduced defect in the authorized 74-check contract. Static inspection and the new production tests establish the following scoped behavior.

`validateExecution` bounds the persisted document, validates the workflow/reachable nodes, limits pending/trace structures, checks invocation and Merge-input shape, detects duplicate/conflicting queued routing invocations and validates waiting dates/deferred Wait routes (`native.mjs` lines 196–213). With a **reachable Merge**, `validateRoutingQueues` compares each pending or buffered batch to the recorded completed immediate predecessor output (lines 215–230). Missing arrived inputs, a substituted batch, a premature downstream effect and incomplete Choose Branch input reject. A deferred Wait's output is intentionally excluded from arrived inputs until resume routes it. Genuine partial joins, full queued Merge behind an independent Wait, and genuine remaining fan-out resume successfully in the finite regression fixtures.

These are structural consistency checks, not checkpoint authentication. A person able to rewrite the workflow, completed outputs and queue together can produce mutually consistent new state. The validator does not prove a complete historical graph prefix, authenticated trace, exact original item-linking history or that remote effects were previously executed. The additional immediate-predecessor checks are gated to workflows with a reachable Merge; they are not advertised as a new universal authenticity check for all persisted linear workflows. Equality here is JSON serialization equality, including property-order/JSON-normalization boundaries, rather than a comparison of arbitrary object reference identity. Trusted local records and existing workflow bounds remain assumptions.

The native server validates recovered records before scheduling them and validates again in `begin` before active execution (`native-server.mjs` lines 11 and 15). The actual current shared HTTP-host test supplied a reachable premature Effect to persisted recovery: the first native API bootstrap returned **HTTP 400**, issued **zero downstream requests**, and left the persisted record byte representation unchanged. This is an actual isolated loopback server/store test, not only a mocked validator. Ready/running interrupted records are not automatically replayed, and waiting records resume through validated scheduling. It still does not provide a remote side-effect transaction, rollback or exactly-once effects after arbitrary host/storage failure.

`advanceExecution` checks cancellation and cumulative active deadline before dispatch; after the awaited checkpoint it checks them again before calling `executeNode` (`native.mjs` line 270). Cancellation retains precedence over the expired active deadline. The expired checkpoint fixture consumed over 120,000 recorded active milliseconds, failed `NATIVE_LIMIT` and invoked zero HTTP requests. The cancellation-during-checkpoint test also invoked zero HTTP requests. The checkpoint I/O itself is awaited cooperatively; neither test establishes preemption of a permanently blocked filesystem operation or a hard real-time budget. Post-node checkpoints, final checkpoints and Wait transitions preserve existing persistence ordering. Their source checks do not authenticate external storage.

HTTP prepares and validates every input request before the node starts any effect, limiting aggregate preparation bytes (`native.mjs` lines 293–305). Each input-item attempt is charged to the 500-attempt budget before its guard, including an item rejected before fetch (lines 307–310). A guard checks cancellation/active deadline before an item, then cancellation/active/per-item timeout before every initial or redirected fetch (lines 306–316). Redirect responses are cancelled before the next hop, each item retains the same timeout across its hops, and 0–10 redirects per item remain a separate cap. The 500 count is **input-item attempts**, not a 500-total-network-hop claim.

Five fresh synthetic HTTP observations were checked independently: expired-before-first gives 0 fetch calls/1 charged attempt; expired-between-items gives 1 call/2 charged attempts; expired-before-redirect gives 1 call/1 attempt; cancellation-before-redirect gives 1 call/1 attempt and `NATIVE_CANCELLED`; timeout-before-redirect gives 1 call/1 attempt and `NATIVE_HTTP_TIMEOUT`. These isolated guard fixtures replace Fetch and a monotonic clock where indicated. They prove guard placement with the given fixtures, not arbitrary blocked-I/O preemption. Existing loopback HTTP tests exercise actual method/query/body/status, response size, timeout and cancellation behavior as part of the same 74 run.

Initial URLs/request bytes/header names/values and response bodies/aggregate outputs retain the existing bounds. Streaming response data is capped at 1 MiB per item and execution output at 8 MiB/10,000 items; supported UTF-8 JSON/text results preserve item pairing within this slice (`native.mjs` lines 319–330). The per-item abort timer is cleared and the external abort listener removed in `finally`. There is no new network destination allowlist or OS memory/process sandbox. Sync preparation, JavaScript processing, persistence and user code executing in the same event loop are not interruptible by these cooperative checks. No credentials/accounts/providers, general expressions, binary items, SQL, complete scheduler/editor or arbitrary graph generation are added.

## Independent run bounds and retained history

The new runner retains stdout at most 8 MiB, stderr at most 2 MiB, a 90-second focused execution deadline, original observations at most 8 MiB, and each synthetic observation at most 128 KiB. Actual stdout was 24,351 bytes and stderr was empty. It read fresh app-owned `.local/recovery-observations/{recovery-observations,http-boundary-observations}.json`, copied their exact bytes and checked all recorded counters; those files are ignored synthetic outputs, not durable historical receipts. The tests use ephemeral own loopback ports and owned temporary stores; no root user host/browser or real data was used.

The outer runner's failure cleanup sends a signal to its owned root only and was not exercised. A complete successful child close plus the tests' normal host/session cleanup is established here, not atomic Windows descendant cleanup under all failures. Filesystem fingerprints, evidence reads and sequential publication writes have no dedicated end-to-end I/O deadline or multi-file transaction. Before/after hashes establish endpoint byte equality, not continuous immutability against transient edits restored between checks. These boundaries do not invalidate the successful finite run but limit broader claims.

Only the new public follow-up receipt/run artifacts and private work files were written. Runtime, shared server/catalog/storage, app documents, first 56-check receipt, original archives and old 721/340 preserved snapshots remained untouched. There were no failed focused test runs to discard; preliminary harness inspection corrected the expected number of recorded recovery observations to five before any execution. No additional diagnostics-only run, browser, full suite, geometry, timing, memory or performance measurement was performed. The independent final `review-validation.json` binds exact current receipt, private sources, public artifacts and this report for root's separate preservation.
